# Compliance and Defensive Perimeter

## Purpose

Defensive perimeter only - no offensive tooling in repo. Documents how auto-blocks enter BPF maps, tarpit rules, and banned imports for compliance changes.

## Auto-block pipeline

Auto-blocks enter BPF maps only after local breach:

| Trigger | Path |
| :--- | :--- |
| Rate limit breach | Edge Lua -> control outbox |
| Operator RBAC block | Admin API -> outbox |
| Fraud outbox event | Control worker -> Redis shard 0 |

TTL in map value; allowlist before deny. Sync path: control outbox -> Redis -> `edge-bpf-sync` -> pinned maps.

Workers do **not** open outbound streams to visitor IPs being blocked. No SYN/UDP flood helpers.

## Tarpit

| Rule | Detail |
| :--- | :--- |
| Surface | Nginx edge when `EDGE_TARPIT_ENABLED=true` |
| Duration | Capped |
| Forbidden | Billing/settlement paths |

## Banned imports

Using `cilium/ebpf` in tracker/control hot packages for compliance maps - edge binaries only (`cmd/edge-xdp`, `cmd/edge-bpf-sync`).

## Change checklist

When editing edge/compliance code:

| Check | Requirement |
| :--- | :--- |
| Outbound | No outbound network to visitor IPs from control workers |
| Map updates | Only via sync pipeline - not direct management kernel writes |
| Metrics | Update metrics and ops docs when adding deny reasons |
| License | Enterprise XDP requires `features.ebpf_xdp_edge` in JWT |

## Verification

